From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 20:36:41 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: compile a pipeline library's stages when it is
 created

VK_EXT_graphics_pipeline_library promises that linking libraries is cheap
next to building them, and KosmicKrisp answered its fast-linking property
with false because every stage was compiled at the link: a library only
serialized its NIR, and the link lowered it, generated the MSL, built an
MTLLibrary per stage and then the MTLRenderPipelineState.  Metal's front
end is the expensive half of that - about 4 ms for one small stage on an
M1 Ultra against 7 ms for the whole pipeline state - so the conformance
test's link-time comparison measured a link 230 times the cost of the
libraries that carried its shaders.

Compile each library's stages when the library is created instead, and
keep the MTLLibrary Metal builds from each one in a device cache keyed by
the source text it was built from.  The link regenerates the same MSL and
finds Metal's compile already done, so it builds only the render pipeline
state.  Nothing but identical source can share an entry, and an entry
lives exactly as long as the libraries that registered it.

A library never sees the chain it will be linked into, so it cannot take
part in cross-stage varying optimization: a fast link optimizes each
stage on its own, exactly as the libraries did, and leaves the interface
where the application declared it.  That is what makes the precompiled
source reusable, and it is also what the extension intends - a link with
VK_PIPELINE_CREATE_LINK_TIME_OPTIMIZATION_BIT_EXT, and every monolithic
pipeline, still links the whole chain.

Metal is the authority on an interface it has never seen joined: it
requires every fragment input to be written by the vertex stage under the
same name and the same type, and Vulkan lets a fragment shader read three
components of a four-component varying.  A refused fast link therefore
falls back to linking the chain the way a monolithic pipeline does, which
narrows the vertex output and matches.  The refusal is expected on that
path, so the bridge takes a flag that keeps a speculative pipeline build
from reporting it.

State a library does not own yet is filled with the values that select
Metal's state-independent path - Metal's own vertex fetch, its
fixed-function blend, no raster emulation, no multiview, one sample.  A
link whose real state agrees regenerates exactly that source; a link that
disagrees produces different source, misses the cache and compiles the
stage itself.  A wrong guess costs a precompile, never correctness.

The link-time test now seeds each library's specialization constant
uniquely per run, because Metal keeps a persistent on-disk cache of the
functions it has already built and reusing the constants measured that
cache instead of this driver.  It also links and draws a four-component
varying against a fragment shader reading all four of it and against one
reading three, which is the interface Metal refuses.  Worst ratio falls
from 230.35 to 0.63 against a limit of 10, and the narrowed interface
renders green where it previously failed the link with
VK_ERROR_INVALID_SHADER_NV.

Keeping each stage's NIR past its serialization moves the duty to free it.
The compile consumes every input NIR whether or not it succeeds, so a
deferral that fails part-way frees all of them, including the stages it had
already serialized, not only the ones it had not reached.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
